00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
hi so today i want to talk a little bit  about motivation and if you clicked on  this video i'm guessing you probably ran  out of motivation so i want to give you  three tips for both long-term motivation  and short-term motivation to keep them  up and to for you to  continue in your journey towards your  phd  so i'm charlotte fraser a second year  phd student in computational  neuroscience  and i've been seeing around me that a  lot of my fellow phd students  do struggle a lot with motivation myself  included so that's why i wanted to dive  into this topic today so the first thing  i want to make really clear is that for  me there's a super distinct separation  between long-term motivation and  short-term motivation so short-term  motivation is this motivation that we  feel that we really want to start our  project and keep on going etc etc but  this type of motivation is by my  experience really fickle so it could be  that it's there some days but it could  also be that you have to work without it  and one thing that i realized pretty  quickly in my phd is that it's not  necessarily motivation that gets the  work done but it's more discipline and  habits so in the book atomic habits by  james clear  he talks about these atomic habits that  people should have every day to make  progress  and once i read this book i really  started to think about an atomic habit  that i want to set for my phd  so i decided that the most important  thing for me to finish my phd  is to do my coding every single day so  my coding work is the main part of my  phd so i try to block in a focus block  of around  three hours every day and really only  work on coding so i'm only allowed to do  that i'm not allowed to do research or  lectures or read some papers  because although this is really  important i think  during your phd it's so easy to just get  lost in this and just to  keep on doing this kind of  procrastination work and feeling really  productive but in the end of the day you  haven't accomplished something  so think about what your main part of  your thesis is going to be about and it  could be coding or it could be  literature research or it could be  doing some data experiments  and try to make those the main focus of  your day and on a related note my second  tip is to  keep these short term goals so try to  when you have your entire phd in front  of you it may seem like this super big  monster but trust me if you cut it up  into tiny parts it is doable  so try to think of what you can do  this week this month and today and  really try to make  your entire phd as small as possible so  really divide it up into tiny chunks and  see what can i do today  and how can i make the best of my day or  time today and i think by doing this you  kinda  relax a little bit and allow yourself to  just work without stressing too much  about the entire product  and my last tip is more related to  stress  so for me personally when i'm under a  lot of stress i actually have a hard  time thinking clearly so for me it works  a lot better to just take a step back  go for a walk or go for a run do a  little exercise when i feel i cannot  focus or have  no motivation to focus  so i think if you are feeling really  negative things it's okay to take a step  back and to just  see your research from a distance  and by taking a walk for example and  just thinking it over in a different  environment you perhaps find the  solution that you're stuck on or refined  your motivation to keep on working  towards your  next tiny goal  so yeah secondly i want to talk a little  bit about long-term motivation  and i think long-term motivation is very  much correlated to our  why so why are we doing our phd why did  we start what do we want to do after and  all these more existential kind of  thoughts  so until now i've seen different types  of reasons why people lose their  long-term motivation and perhaps you can  recognize yourself in one of them and i  will try to give you a solution so the  first one i want to talk about is  imposter syndrome so imposter syndrome  is this  deep sense or this feeling that you're  somewhere where you're actually not  allowed to be  or you're faking it and that people will  slowly uncover the secrets of your  identity and  will see that you're actually a false or  a fake type of person and i think this  is really prevalent amongst researchers  because our field is changing so much  and it's so  the knowledge that we have to have  is so vast nowadays that there's always  gaps  in our knowledge and i think as phd  students we finally become really aware  of what we know and what we don't know  so for me personally i came from a  background of physics  and then i studied more  artificial intelligence and a little bit  of programming and finally i transferred  towards full neuroscience but because of  this background i always felt that i was  never really good at  any one of them and all my fellow phd  students in my field have a similar type  of scattered  background so they feel very similar  that they're or not very good at  programming or they're not very good at  the neuroscience  stuff or they're not very familiar with  the biology  so i think because as a phd student you  finally realize  how much you don't know  you feel this sense of imposter syndrome  and for me a solution towards this was  just kind of accepting  that i feel like this so just accepting  that i sometimes don't feel the smartest  in the room or that i sometimes feel  that my professor may think that i don't  know as much as he hopes i know and i  think once you accept this  you will also let go a little bit of the  fear that people will find out  and also it can be a motivator to learn  a lot more so if you really feel you are  not knowledgeable enough in a certain  field it could be a problem for you to  start  working more towards that field  but from my experience and also talking  with people that are a bit further down  the line this fear of imposter syndrome  never really goes away so if you have it  and you're struggling with it  just accept it and just imagine that  everyone around you feels the same and i  think this already can give some relief  and the second type of long-term  motivation problems that i see  are more related to our  why we started i think the reason why we  start at the beginning our phd can be  very different  than the reason why we continue  so for me the reason that i started and  i think for a lot of people it's similar  is that i did want to become perhaps a  professor  or continue in the field of  the university track towards  professorship but doing research and  working in this more hierarchical  field i kind of realized that this was  not what i want to be doing because  professors are actually not doing that  much research they're mostly writing  letters for funding or checking other  people's work  so they're actually not doing that much  research themselves they're more  thinking about big ideas or managing  and i kind of realized quite soon that  this wasn't what i wanted for my future  so i really had to rethink what my phd  could bring  for me and for my life  and rethinking this i kind of know now  that i want to do  more on outreach and more towards these  type of educational videos  or perhaps go into a really big research  team where i work with more people  because a phd journey is a pretty  lonely journey but i still think my phd  can give me  a lot of credit  and a lot of opportunity to learn so  many skills that will be helpful for my  future  so for example because i know i want to  do a little bit more with outreach and  also the border between ai and  society  i'm gonna go with a friend of mine to  this conference about ai and democracy  and i think for me by realizing what i  want to do in the future without with my  phd and also make better choices right  now as to what i want to do right now  and the last point i also want to touch  upon why you are maybe struggling with  your long-term motivation during your  phd is if you're really tired or really  anxious for multiple days and you also  have this tied up with your lack of  motivation these could actually be the  first signs of a burnout  and if you're feeling like this i would  really recommend you to reach out  because the problem may not be something  solvable by yourself but you kind of  need a system or  some help from the outside to guide you  towards more healthy habits or a  healthier workplace because it could  also be something outside of you that is  causing this stress and i think burnout  is just such a detrimental feeling for  us because it really is not something  that can be solved immediately and i  also made a video about it so if you are  feeling like this maybe you can check it  out and see  if that can help you a little bit but  again i want to reiterate do reach out  to someone  and talk to  your friends your family or perhaps even  a therapist so yeah please stay safe and  healthy take care of yourself  and if you have any tips or tricks for  me as to how to deal with motivation i  would love to hear them and put them in  the comments down below  and see you next time  bye
 

